    "evidence_text": "This suggests that a model's ability to capture generalizable structure is highly sensitive.\n\nMoreover, this sensitivity may not be apparent when evaluating it on standard test sets.\n\nWe refer to this notion as linguistic generalization .\n\nMany problems in NLP are treated as sequence to sequence tasks with solutions built on seq2seq-attention based models.\n\nHowever, learning structure is not a sufficient condition to achieving linguistic generalization.\n\nOne may hope, given the right hyperparameter\n\nIn particular, we train models on a symbol replacement task with a well defined generalizable structure.\n\nGeneralization in a Symbol Rewriting Task\tReal\n\nAs done in previous work  ,  , we ease analysis by looking at a simple formal task.\n\ncharacteristic inputs to decide the epoch to stop at.\n\nAll other hyperparameters were also decided using this validation set.\n\nFurther training details are listed in Table 1 .\n\nin a single sample.\n\nThe second set tests the model's ability to generalize to repeated symbols in the input (Repeat).\n\nFurther work explored how RNNs can learn context free\n\nlanguages  ,  as well some context sensitive languages  .\n\nhas questioned the systematicity with which these models learn.\n\nThe same work also makes similar observations about the difficulty of generalizing to longer length strings.\n\ngeneralization.",
